Package: RcmdrShiny
Type: Package
Title: A Browser-Native Statistical Workbench Inspired by the R Commander
Version: 2.8.0
Authors@R: c(
    person("RcmdrShiny", "Contributors", role = c("aut", "cre"),
           email = "vigliafg@gmail.com"),
    person("John", "Fox", role = "ctb",
           comment = "Author of Rcmdr, from which this package is derived"),
    person("Milan", "Bouchet-Valat", role = "ctb",
           comment = "Author of Rcmdr, from which this package is derived")
  )
Description: A local, browser-based statistical workbench inspired by the
    R Commander (Rcmdr).  The package keeps the command-generation semantics
    of Rcmdr while removing the dependency on Tcl/Tk, so that every action in
    the interface produces a reproducible R command.  The Studio interface
    adds a command palette, structured command history, a Data Studio with
    data set registry, column inspector, reproducible structural operations,
    checkpoints and an import preview, a Chart Studio with a visual builder,
    isolated live preview, themed commands, a searchable gallery and a
    round-trip back to the builder, a Report Studio with a document registry,
    outline, fast Markdown preview, executable cells, templates, conservative
    YAML editing, a knit manager and autosave/recovery, a Workbench with
    multi-document scripts, executable cells, an R console with recall
    history, filterable output and message cards, an object explorer with
    reproducible actions, a model registry with a comparison dashboard, a
    navigation layer with an advanced command palette, favourites, recents,
    menu search, dialog presets and inline validation, guided workflows that
    orchestrate existing dialogs, and contextual variable suggestions, an
    offline help centre with a full-text index, filters, runnable examples,
    contextual error-to-topic links, a glossary and a first-run tour, plus a
    live language switch, a curated multi-level menubar with stable menu and
    item identifiers, and a classic compatibility mode.
